From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hunter B Hough <git@hough.ink>
Date: Thu, 15 Oct 2026 15:33:04 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 180/181] feat(gastown): ship the polecat resume re-verify and
 already-submitted guard as pack executables (gcp-wmcj9)

The two remaining large fences in the polecat prompt (the 64-line resume /
crash re-verify and the 70-line FINAL REMINDER already-submitted guard, plus
its rendered duplicate in approval-fallacy.template.md) were retyped by the
model every session. They now run as pack executables, the way gcp-zzb0 ships
GC_CLAIM, shrinking the rendered polecat prompt from 38,526 to 30,451 bytes.

polecat-resume-verify.sh takes no arguments and prints exactly one of
RESUME_VERIFIED, OWNERSHIP_LOST, RESUME_INDETERMINATE. polecat-already-
submitted.sh takes the binding prefix as its one required argument (it was a
{{ .BindingPrefix }} substitution inside the fence) and prints
ALREADY_SUBMITTED then drain-acks only on positive evidence. Prompt and
fragment keep a short stub in the polecat-claim.sh form: a -f check on the
script under {{ .ConfigDir }}. A missing resume script fails closed
(RESUME_INDETERMINATE plus drain-ack); a missing already-submitted script does
not drain and falls through to submit-and-exit, since no evidence means submit.
claim_assignee_is_ours stays a copy, not a shared library, to keep the
race-critical claim path free of a locate-the-library failure; the drift test
pins the copies byte-identical.

Behaviour is identical to the old fences (the fragment echo gains a
"submit-and-exit already ran;" suffix shared by both callers). Tests that
extracted or pinned fence text now read the scripts; new
test_polecat_already_submitted.sh covers 11 behavioural cases.

Validation: applied from the seat-verified artifact (sha256 a19330de...);
test_polecat_resume_reverify, test_polecat_already_submitted,
test_gastown_pack_assets, test_polecat_claim and test_polecat_live_owner_guard
are run before handoff.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
